argin:0;color:var(--accent);font-size:38px;letter-spacing:-.04em}.model{margin:8px 0 0;color:#667085}.sheet-body{padding:24px}.table-wrap{overflow:auto}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;min-width:900px}th,td{padding:12px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);text-align:left;vertical-align:top;font-size:14px}th{background:rgba(37,26,94,.08);color:var(--accent);text-transform:uppercase;font-size:12px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.06em}.section-divider td{background:linear-gradient(90deg,rgba(37,26,94,.12),rgba(243,151,14,.08))!important;color:var(--accent);font-weight:900;font-size:13px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em;border-top:3px solid rgba(243,151,14,.35);padding:14px 12px}tr:nth-child(even) td{background:rgba(243,151,14,.03)}footer{margin-top:26px;text-align:center;color:#667085;font-size:14px}@media(max-width:860px){header.hero{grid-template-columns:1fr}.page{width:min(100% - 20px,720px)}.quick-card{max-height:none}table{min-width:760px}}
</style></head><body><div class="page"><header class="hero"><section class="hero-card"><h1>Test Planning</h1><p class="subtitle">QR-friendly overview of planned tests and validation activities. COMPONENTS / SUBSYSTEMS dividers are shown only when both levels are present in the same subsystem.</p><div class="meta-row"><span class="pill">Preliminary Design Review</span><span class="pill">May 2026</span><span class="pill">65 tests</span></div></section><aside class="quick-card"><h2>Quick access</h2><nav class="nav-grid"><a class="nav-link" href="#sheet-0"><span><strong>SYSTEM</strong><span>22 tests</span></span><span class="arrow">↓</span></a>
<a class="nav-link" href="#sheet-1"><span><strong>ADCS</strong><span>12 tests</span></span><span class="arrow">↓</span></a>
<a class="nav-link" href="#sheet-2"><span><strong>EPS</strong><span>10 tests</span></span><span class="arrow">↓</span></a>
<a class="nav-link" href="#sheet-3"><span><strong>TTC</strong><span>10 tests</span></span><span class="arrow">↓</span></a>
<a class="nav-link" href="#sheet-4"><span><strong>PROP</strong><span>10 tests</span></span><span class="arrow">↓</span></a>
<a class="nav-link" href="#sheet-5"><span><strong>THERMO-STRUCTURE</strong><span>1 tests</span></span><span class="arrow">↓</span></a></nav></aside></header><main><article class="datasheet" id="sheet-0"><div class="sheet-head"><h2>SYSTEM</h2><p class="model">22 test entries</p></div><div class="sheet-body"><div class="table-wrap"><table class="styled-table"><thead><tr><th>TYPE OF TEST</th><th>TEST NAME</th><th>ITEM VERIFIED</th><th>DESCRIPTION</th><th>REQ</th><th>EXPECTED RESULTS</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interference</td><td>RF Compatibility Testing (RFCT)</td><td></td><td>Direct loop-back and over-the-air communication testing between the onboard TT&amp;C transponder and ground station network simulators (ESTRACK and NASA DSN). This verifies nominal uplink command reception, downlink telemetries, and ranging lock profiles at maximum operational distances.</td><td>F.8<br>P.8<br>TTC_D.4<br>TTC_D.4.1<br>TTC_D.5</td><td>F.8 The space segment shall verify the link budget with the Deep Space Network.<br>P.8 The TT&amp;C antennas (S/X-band or optical) shall close the communication link budget with the DSN while subject to a spacecraft pointing error below ±0.1° [TBC]. <br>TTC_D.4 The TTC subsystem shall transmit spacecraft telemetry to Earth.<br>TTC_D.4.1 The TTC subsystem shall support a minimum telemetry data rate of ≥ 128 kbps in nominal conditions and ≥ 1 Mbps for Precise Orbit Determination (POD). <br>TTC_D.5 The TTC subsystem shall support command uplink from Earth GS.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>LunaNet PNT Signal Interoperability Verification</td><td></td><td>Software and signal-level validation of the S-band navigation signal. Broadcast messages will be decoded by standard LunaNet-compliant user receiver testbeds to prove that POLPO data structures (ephemerides, clock parameters and time-tags) seamlessly interoperate with NASA and JAXA lunar surface assets.</td><td>F.2<br>P.2.1<br>P.2.2<br>P.3.3<br>F.6<br>TTC_D.1.3</td><td>F.2 The space segment shall provide PNT for the lunar surface.<br>P.2.1 The constellation shall provide PNT with at least 100m horizontal position accuracy for the lunar surface.<br>P.2.2 The constellation shall provide PNT with at least 200m vertical position accuracy for the lunar surface. <br>P.3.3 The constellation shall ensure coverage with time-to-convergence of at maximum 10 minutes. <br>F.6 The navigation system shall support external ranging sources (e.g. lunar surface pseudolites) when available.<br>TTC_D.1.3 The time synchronization error relative to the lunar reference time shall be ≤ 100 ns [TBC].</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Dynamic ISL Handover Hardware-in-the-Loop (HIL)</td><td></td><td>Dynamic network test utilizing an ADCS simulator to drive the physical APM gimbal motors. This simulates the rapid relative velocity and angular changes between the 8 Equatorial and 16 HALO satellites. It verifies the capability of the KARMA-7 antenna to perform continuous autonomous Two-Way Measurements and seamless data handovers without losing synchronization.</td><td>F.9</td><td>F.9 The space segment shall establish an intersatellite link.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Inter-Satellite Link (ISL) Antenna Pointing</td><td></td><td>Verify that the ADCS can orient the spacecraft to maintain antenna pointing required for ISL.<br>Put a RF link simulator or second spacecraft simulator. Insert orbital dynamics simulator to propagate the orbit simulate relative orbital positions between two satellites, generate target line-of-sight vectors for inter-satellite communication, command the ADCS to track the target satellite. <br>Desired measurements: <br>- Antenna boresight pointing error <br>- Tracking stability <br>- Link acquisition time <br>- Link maintenance duration <br>Repeat for different relative geometries and motion profiles.</td><td>F.9<br>F.11.3<br>TTC_D.1.2</td><td>F.9 The space segment shall establish an intersatellite link.<br>F.11.3 ADCS should provide pointing capability for intersatellite link.<br>TTC_D.1.2 The design of the TTC antennas shall be compatible with the required pointing capabilities of the ADCS defined at P.11.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Thruster-based RWs desaturation test</td><td></td><td>Verify that the ADCS correctly uses thrusters to unload RWs momentum when saturation is reached. <br>Procedure:<br>- Operate spacecraft in closed-loop attitude control mode. <br>- Apply continuous disturbance torque to drive reaction wheels toward saturation. <br>- Monitor wheel momentum buildup. <br>- Activate desaturation logic. <br>- Verify ADCS commands thruster firings to reduce wheel momentum. <br>- Observe attitude stability during firing events.</td><td>ADCS_ D.13 <br>PROP_D.1.2<br>PROP_D.7.2</td><td>ADCS_D.13 ADCS shall provide an external actuator to avoid saturation of internal actuators. <br>PROP_D.1.2 The Propulsive System shall be able to perform the desaturation of the internal actuators. <br>PROP_D.7.2 The Propulsion System shall be able to provide a DELTA-V of 8 m/s for desaturation of the internal actuators.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Thruster Pairing and Torque Balance</td><td></td><td>To verify correct generation of pure torques using thruster pairs. <br>Procedure:<br>- Command attitude torque using thruster pairs (opposite forces).<br> - Verify torque vector direction and magnitude. <br>- Repeat for all axes combinations. <br>- Monitor unintended translational forces.</td><td>ADCS_ D.13</td><td>ADCS_D.13 ADCS shall provide an external actuator to avoid saturation of internal actuators.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Thruster Failure Impact on ADCS</td><td></td><td>To verify ADCS robustness under partial thruster failure. <br>Procedure:<br>- Simulate failure of one or more thrusters (stuck OFF / stuck ON / reduced thrust).<br> - Execute desaturation and attitude control maneuvers. <br>- Evaluate control reconfiguration response</td><td>ADCS_ D.13</td><td>ADCS_D.13 ADCS shall provide an external actuator to avoid saturation of internal actuators.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Thrusters attitude control</td><td></td><td>To verify the ability of thrusters to substitute internal actuators in providing the torque to maintain reuqired attitude (failure of internal actuators).</td><td>PROP_D.7.3</td><td>PROP_D.7.3The Propulsion System shall be able to provide a DELTA-V of 2 m/s for attitude control in case of failure of the internal actuators.</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanical</td><td>Launcher adapter fit-check</td><td></td><td>Perform mechanical fit-check and compatibility verification with the launch vehicle adapter (LVA), evaluating structural interfaces, clearances, accessibility, connector mating, and integration constraints.</td><td>TSS_D.1<br>TSS_D.5</td><td>TSS_D.1: Spacecraft stowed envelope shall fit within [1*1*2] m including appendages and tolerances.<br>TSS_D.5: The spacecraft structure shall provide mechanical mounting interfaces for payload and subsystems per Interface Control Document (ICD).</td></tr><tr><td>Vibrational Tests</td><td>Modal survey test</td><td></td><td>Perform modal identification (natural frequencies, mode shapes, and damping ratios) for structural FEM correlation. Conduct low-level signature sweeps prior to and following each mechanical test run.</td><td>TSS_D.4.2</td><td>TSS_D.4.2: First natural frequencies in launch configuration shall meet launcher requirements &gt; 20 Hz.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Quasi-static load test</td><td></td><td>Perform structural qualification under launch-equivalent quasi-static loads. Certification may be achieved through a combined test and analysis approach, subject to program tailoring.</td><td>TSS_D.2<br>TSS_D.4.1</td><td>TSS_D.2: Design factors of safety for primary structure shall be ≥ [1.10] (yield) and ≥ [1.25] (ultimate) per applicable standard [TBC].<br>TSS_D.4.1: The spacecraft structure shall withstand quasi-static launch loads of ±[10] g axial and ±[8] g lateral with positive Margin of Safety for yield and ultimate.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Sine vibration test</td><td></td><td>Perform low-frequency sine vibration testing to evaluate spacecraft response, simulating equivalent quasi-static loads and global launch vehicle dynamics.</td><td>TSS_D.4<br>TSS_D.4.2</td><td>TSS_D.4: The spacecraft shall be qualified for the launch dynamic environment per launcher specification per GSFC-STD-7000 (GEVS) [TBC] (sine, random vibration, acoustic, shock).<br>TSS_D.4.2: First natural frequencies in launch configuration shall meet launcher requirements &gt; 20 Hz.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Random vibration test</td><td></td><td>Perform random vibration testing to simulate the launch vehicle broadband environment, conducted along the orthogonal spacecraft axes or in accordance with launcher-specific configurations.</td><td>TSS_D.4</td><td>TSS_D.4: The spacecraft shall be qualified for the launch dynamic environment per launcher specification per GSFC-STD-7000 (GEVS) [TBC] (sine, random vibration, acoustic, shock).</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Acoustic Test</td><td></td><td>Acoustic environmental testing is more representative than shaker table excitation for large-scale spacecraft, as launch acoustic loads directly excite structural panels, deployable appendages, and large-area surfaces.</td><td>TSS_D.4</td><td>TSS_D.4: The spacecraft shall be qualified for the launch dynamic environment per launcher specification per GSFC-STD-7000 (GEVS) [TBC] (sine, random vibration, acoustic, shock).</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Shock / pyroshock test</td><td></td><td>Perform shock qualification testing to verify the structural and functional integrity of electronics, harness, clocks, RF payload, and mechanisms subjected to separation and release shock loads.</td><td>TSS_D.4</td><td>TSS_D.4: The spacecraft shall be qualified for the launch dynamic environment per launcher specification per GSFC-STD-7000 (GEVS) [TBC] (sine, random vibration, acoustic, shock).</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Deployment test post-vibration</td><td></td><td>Conduct post-environmental mechanical testing (vibration, acoustic, and pyroshock) to verify the functional performance and deployment mechanisms of stowed appendages, including antennas nd solar arrays.</td><td>TSS_D.1<br>TSS_D.4<br>TSS_D.5</td><td>TSS_D.1: Spacecraft stowed envelope shall fit within [1*1*2] m including appendages and tolerances.<br>TSS_D.4: The spacecraft shall be qualified for the launch dynamic environment per launcher specification per GSFC-STD-7000 (GEVS) [TBC] (sine, random vibration, acoustic, shock).<br>TSS_D.5: The spacecraft structure shall provide mechanical mounting interfaces for payload and subsystems per Interface Control Document (ICD).</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Alignment / metrology after mechanical vibrational tests</td><td></td><td>Post-test alignment of antennas, phase centers, star trackers, IMU and reference frames<br>Verifica che antenne, phase center, star tracker, IMU e reference frame non abbiano subito disallineamenti oltre tolleranza.</td><td>TSS_D.4<br>TSS_D.5</td><td>TSS_D.4: The spacecraft shall be qualified for the launch dynamic environment per launcher specification per GSFC-STD-7000 (GEVS) [TBC] (sine, random vibration, acoustic, shock).<br>TSS_D.5: The spacecraft structure shall provide mechanical mounting interfaces for payload and subsystems per Interface Control Document (ICD).</td></tr><tr><td>Thermo-vacuum Tests</td><td>Thermal Balance Test</td><td></td><td>Perform thermal balance testing for thermal mathematical model (TMM) correlation, including hot/cold steady-state plateaus and characterization of temperature distributions, power dissipations, heater duty cycles, and thermal gradients.</td><td>TSS_D.3.1<br>TSS_D.3.2<br>TSS_D.3.3<br>TSS_D.3.5<br>TSS_D.3.6.1<br>TSS_D.3.6.2</td><td>TSS_D.3.1: The TCS shall monitor spacecraft temperatures and provide them as telemetry.<br>TSS_D.3.2: Temperature measurement accuracy shall be ≤ ±1.5°C over the operational sensor range [-90°C - 115°C] at EOL [TBC].<br>TSS_D.3.3: Temperature measurement shall be performed at a minimal frequency of 1 Hz.<br>TSS_D.3.5: The TCS shall maintain structural temperatures within [-40°C +80°C] at defined reference points [TBC] on the primary structure, equipment panels, and thermo-elastic control locations, for worst hot/cold cases [TBC] including BOL/EOL.<br>TSS_D.3.6.1: The TCS shall maintain structural temperatures within [-40°C +80°C] at structural reference points [TBC], including critical unit mounting interfaces, navigation antenna support interfaces, clock support interfaces, radiator interfaces, for the mission worst hot and worst cold cases TBC.<br>TSS_D.3.6.2: Payload spatial temperature gradient shall be ≤ [1°C/cm] across baseline [TBC] during steady-state payload operation.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Mechanism thermo-vacuum deployment test</td><td></td><td>Perform deployment mechanism functional testing under thermal-vacuum (TVAC) extremes for deployable antennas and solar arrays, verifying that low-temperature environments do not induce lubricant degradation or hinge seizure.</td><td>TTC_D.1.4<br>TSS_D.1<br>TSS_D.3<br>TSS_D.5</td><td>TTC_D.1.4 The satellite shall deploy lunar nadir-pointing antenna. <br>TSS_D.1: Spacecraft stowed envelope shall fit within [1*1*2] m including appendages and tolerances.<br>TSS_D.3: The space segment shall be provided with a Thermal Control System (TCS).<br>TSS_D.5: The spacecraft structure shall provide mechanical mounting interfaces for payload and subsystems per Interface Control Document (ICD).</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Outgassing /contamination detection</td><td></td><td>Perform molecular contamination assessment and material outgassing verification under vacuum, critical for thermal control surfaces, antennas, optical sensors, and contamination-sensitive components.</td><td>TSS_D.3.11</td><td>TSS_D.3.11: External thermal control materials (coatings/MLI/tapes) shall comply with outgassing requirements per standard [TBD].</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Thermal transient test</td><td></td><td>Perform integrated system-level verification during eclipse/sunlight transitions, payload activation transients, attitude slew maneuvers, and safe mode recovery operations.</td><td>TSS_D.3.1<br>TSS_D.3.3<br>TSS_D.3.4<br>TSS_D.3.10<br>TSS_O.1</td><td>TSS_D.3.1: The TCS shall monitor spacecraft temperatures and provide them as telemetry.<br>TSS_D.3.3: Temperature measurement shall be performed at a minimal frequency of 1 Hz.<br>TSS_D.3.4: The TCS shall actively control spacecraft thermal state by commanding heaters.<br>TSS_D.3.10: The design shall provide survival heating capability for critical units [TBD], with autonomous activation architecture [TBD].<br>TSS_O.1 TCS shall support defined mission operational modes (e.g., LEOP, Nominal, Standby, Safe) (mode definition [TBC]).</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Spacecraft-level TVAC</td><td></td><td>Verify the fully integrated system in a thermal-vacuum environment. Objective is to detect integration issues, harness faults, MLI effectiveness, heater control performance, radiative coupling, and real power dissipations. (Mandatory if a facility compatible with the full spacecraft dimensions is available). <br>For large satellites, a few representative cycles are typically performed at the spacecraft level, whereas the most severe and numerous cycles are conducted at the unit/subsystem level.<br>Temperatures vary between survival and operational ranges:<br>- Hot survival case: survival under non-operational hot conditions. Useful for safe mode, off-nominal attitude, or degraded thermal control.<br>- Cold survival case: survival during eclipse, safe mode, power-limited mode, or deep-space cold conditions.<br>- Hot operational case (WHC): GNSS payload activated, maximum power dissipation, and communications operational.<br>- Cold operational case (WCC): verification of avionics, payload, clocks, RF chain, batteries, and thermal control functionality.<br>During hot/cold plateaus, full or partial Functional Tests must be executed, including: EPS, OBC, AOCS, TT&amp;C, GNSS payload, clocks, and RF chains.<br>Conduct post-test physical and visual inspection of spacecraft hardware: verify the status of MLI, radiators, surface coatings, thermal sensors, heater bonding, and electrical harness to detect any structural damage or molecular/particulate contamination.</td><td>TSS_D.3<br>TSS_D.3.5<br>TSS_D.3.6.1<br>TSS_D.3.6.2<br>TSS_D.3.7<br>TSS_D.3.8<br>TSS_D.3.9<br>TSS_D.3.10<br>TSS_O.1<br>TSS_O.1.1</td><td>TSS_D.3: The space segment shall be provided with a Thermal Control System (TCS).<br>TSS_D.3.5: The TCS shall maintain structural temperatures within [-40°C +80°C] at defined reference points [TBC] on the primary structure, equipment panels, and thermo-elastic control locations, for worst hot/cold cases [TBC] including BOL/EOL.<br>TSS_D.3.6.1: The TCS shall maintain structural temperatures within [-40°C +80°C] at structural reference points [TBC], including critical unit mounting interfaces, navigation antenna support interfaces, clock support interfaces, radiator interfaces, for the mission worst hot and worst cold cases TBC.<br>TSS_O.1 TCS shall support defined mission operational modes (e.g., LEOP, Nominal, Standby, Safe) (mode definition [TBC]). <br>TSS_O.1.1 In Safe Mode, the spacecraft shall remain within survival thermal limits for ≥ [72] hours [TBC]. <br>TSS_D.3.6.2: Payload spatial temperature gradient shall be ≤ [1°C/cm] across baseline [TBC] during steady-state payload operation.<br>TSS_D.3.7: The TCS shall maintain Propulsion system temperature within [-30 and +40°C].<br>TSS_D.3.8: The TCS shall maintain EPS temperature within [0°C - 50°C] [TBC].<br>TSS_D.3.9: The spacecraft survival thermal interval in space shall be [-10°C and +60°C] for survival configuration [TBC] and duration [72 h] [TBC].<br>TSS_D.3.10: The design shall provide survival heating capability for critical units [TBD], with autonomous activation architecture [TBD].</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></article>
<article class="datasheet" id="sheet-1"><div class="sheet-head"><h2>ADCS</h2><p class="model">12 test entries</p></div><div class="sheet-body"><div class="table-wrap"><table class="styled-table"><thead><tr><th>TYPE OF TEST</th><th>TEST NAME</th><th>ITEM VERIFIED</th><th>DESCRIPTION</th><th>REQ</th><th>EXPECTED RESULTS</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="section-divider"><td colspan="6">COMPONENTS</td></tr><tr><td>Performance</td><td>Gyroscope ARW detection</td><td>Gyroscopes</td><td>Determine ARW, bias and misalignment in static conditions. <br>Dynamic test with rate table for ARW: determine scale factor and check the low rate locking limit</td><td>ADCS_D.3.2<br>ADCS_D.5</td><td>ADCS_D.3.2 ADCS gyroscopes shall satisfy scale factor error lower than 50 ppm. <br>ADCS_D.5 ADCS sensors shall satisfy static alignment angle error lower than ±5 arcsec .</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Star Tracker ARW detection</td><td>Star Tracker</td><td>Determine ARW and misalignment in static conditions using a fictitious star field.</td><td>ADCS_D.5</td><td>ADS_D.5 ADCS sensors shall satisfy static alignment angle error lower than ±5 arcsec .</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>RW functionality verification</td><td>RWs</td><td>Verify RWs torque generation and speed control <br><br>In nominal conditions:<br>1. Command multiple wheel speeds and torque levels. <br>2. Measure wheel response and stability. <br>3. Verify acceleration/deceleration behavior. <br><br>Approaching saturation:<br>1. Apply continuous disturbance torque. <br>2. Allow reaction wheels to accumulate momentum. <br>3. Observe compensator output (lead control) as saturation is approached. <br>4. Monitor attitude stability.</td><td>F.11<br>ADCS_D.14</td><td>F.11 The satellite shall provide an ADCS system to provide pointing capabilities.<br>ADCS_D.14 ADCS shall minimize the electromagnetic interactions between internal actuator’s poles and windings to reduce torque noise.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Saturation Threshold Detection Test</td><td>RWs</td><td>Verify that the ADCS activates the external actuator only when the defined saturation threshold is reached.<br>Procedure: <br>1. Gradually increase internal actuator load. <br>2. Monitor saturation detection logic. <br>3. Verify actuator activation below, at, and above threshold values.</td><td>ADCS_ D.13</td><td>ADCS_D.13 ADCS shall provide an external actuator to avoid saturation of internal actuators.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Actuator desaturation test</td><td>RWs</td><td>Verify that the ADCS provides an external actuator capability to desaturate the internal actuators and restore nominal operating conditions. It could be not easy because external actuator is by far less accurate than internal ones. <br>Procedure:<br>1. Operate the spacecraft in closed-loop attitude control mode to have continuous monitoring of attitude error. <br>2. Apply disturbance torques to increase internal actuator momentum toward saturation limits. <br>3. Monitor internal actuator momentum buildup and describe it with a certain law. <br>4. Enable the external actuator to discrete desaturation function. <br>5. Observe the ADCS response while momentum unloading is performed</td><td>ADCS_ D.13</td><td>ADCS_D.13 ADCS shall provide an external actuator to avoid saturation of internal actuators.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Maneuver Performance Test</td><td>RWs</td><td>Verify spacecraft maneuver capability between target attitudes.<br>Procedure : <br>1. Command multiple large-angle slews. <br>2. Measure maneuver duration, overshoot, and settling time.</td><td>ADCS_D.2</td><td>ADCS_D.2 Each satellite shall provide Attitude Control capabilities.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Fault Detection and Recovery Test</td><td>RWs</td><td>Verify ADCS fault management capability<br>Procedure: <br>1. Inject representative sensor/actuator faults. <br>2. Observe fault detection, isolation, and recovery actions.</td><td>ADCS_D.2</td><td>ADCS_D.2 Each satellite shall provide Attitude Control capabilities.</td></tr><tr class="section-divider"><td colspan="6">SUBSYSTEMS</td></tr><tr><td>Performance</td><td>Attitude Determination and <br>control convergence Test</td><td>ADC Subsystem</td><td>Verify convergence speed and stability of the full attitude closed loop.<br>Procedure:<br>- Initialize ADCS with large attitude estimation error. <br>- Start sensor fusion algorithm and attitude control loop.<br> - Monitor convergence to true attitude reference.<br> - Repeat for different initial conditions and noise levels.</td><td>ADCS_D.2</td><td>ADCS_D.2 Each satellite shall provide Attitude Control capabilities.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Pointing capability</td><td>ADC Subsystem</td><td>Verify ability to determine and control spacecraft attitute to achieve commanded orientations. <br>Procedure:<br>1.Initialize the spacecraft in a known attitude state, command a sequence of target attitudes on all three axes. <br>2.Introduce representative disturbances and attitude offsets (such as the torque noise, to make the maneuver more realistic). <br>3.While the system perform attitude rotations or maintain commanded orientation, monitor response in terms of:<br>- Pointing accuracy<br>- Pointing stability after manouver<br>- Settling time<br>- Manouver duration <br>- Overshoot<br>4.Repeat for multiple orientations and slew manouvers (in this case monitor also slew rate).</td><td>F11<br>ADCS_D.2<br>ADCS_D.8</td><td>F.11 The satellite shall provide an ADCS system to provide pointing capabilities.<br>ADCS_D.2 Each satellite shall provide Attitude Control capabilities. <br>ADCS_D.8 ADCS sensors shall guarantee angular rate measurements &lt;400 deg/s dynamic range.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Mode Transition Stability Test</td><td>ADC Subsystem</td><td>Verify stability during ADCS mode transitions. <br>Procedure:<br>- Switch between control modes ( lunar pointing → desaturation → sun pointing → earth pointing). <br>- Observe transient behavior during transitions. <br>- Monitor attitude excursions.</td><td>ADCS_D.2</td><td>ADCS_D.2 Each satellite shall provide Attitude Control capabilities.</td></tr><tr><td>Thermo-vacuum</td><td>Instruments calibration</td><td>ADC Subsystem</td><td>Determine attitude determination instruments bias law dependency on temperature in vacuum conditions (RLG, accelerometers, star tracker)</td><td>ADCS_D.4.2</td><td>ADCS_D.4.2 ADCS accelerometers shall satisfy bias error lower than 10 μg.</td></tr><tr><td>Vibrational</td><td>ADCS response to sollectations</td><td>ADC Subsystem</td><td>Critical subsystem: dynamic vibration test of acceleromenters, Star Tracker and RWs.</td><td>ADCS_D.4.1</td><td>ADCS_D.4.1 ADCS accelerometers shall satisfy scale factor error lower than 200 ppm .</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></article>
<article class="datasheet" id="sheet-2"><div class="sheet-head"><h2>EPS</h2><p class="model">10 test entries</p></div><div class="sheet-body"><div class="table-wrap"><table class="styled-table"><thead><tr><th>TYPE OF TEST</th><th>TEST NAME</th><th>ITEM VERIFIED</th><th>DESCRIPTION</th><th>REQ</th><th>EXPECTED RESULTS</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Performance</td><td>Capacity characterization</td><td>Battery</td><td>Misura l&#x27;energia massima contenuta a diverse temperature. <br>Valuta l&#x27;efficienza della batteria: resistenza interna e perdite per effetto Joule.</td><td>P.12.4</td><td>P.12.4 The EPS shall have a battery system with total available electrical energy capacity of 2700 Wh.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Autoscarica</td><td>Battery</td><td>Misura la perdita di carica della batteria quando non è utilizzata.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Functionality</td><td>Battery</td><td>Test della scarica della batteria quando impiegata come sorgente primaria: <br>- inserire i rispettivi subsystem (o strumenti rappresentativi della potenza richiesta da ciascuno) uno alla volta e verificare che i carichi richiesti da singolo subsystem vengano gestiti correttamente dall’EPS.<br>- verificare che ci sia costante flusso energetico anche nelle transizioni tra operation phases</td><td>F.12<br>P.12.1<br>P.12.2<br>P.12.3<br>EPS_D.1.1<br>EPS_D.1.2<br>EPS_D.1.3<br>EPS_D.1.4<br>EPS_D1.5<br>EPS_D.4<br>EPS O.1</td><td>F.12 The spacecraft shall be provided with EPS to provide power to the payload and all the subsystems in every operational phase.<br>P.12.1 The EPS shall provide a peak amount of power of 1280 W.<br>P.12.2 The EPS shall provide an average amount of power of 500 W at any time.<br>P.12.3 The EPS shall provide a minimum power of 300 W± in any mission phase.<br>EPS_D.1.1 The EPS shall provide power to the payload.<br>EPS_D.1.2 The EPS shall provide power to the ADCS. <br>EPS_D.1.3 The EPS shall provide power to the Propulsion System. <br>EPS_D.1.4 The EPS shall provide power for active TCS. <br>EPS_D.1.5 The EPS shall provide power to TTC System. <br>EPS_D.4 The EPS shall provide stable buses to all components in order to ensure a correct power distribution. <br>EPS_O.1 The EPS shall provide electrical power in every nominal operational phase.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Lyfe cycling</td><td>Battery</td><td>Test della degradazione della batteria effettuando cicli di carica/scarica e proiettando la degradazione per il lifecycle di minimo 5 anni considerando un 70% di D.o.D.</td><td>P.12.5</td><td>P.12.5 The EPS shall limit DoD up to a limit of 70% to prevent heavy battery degradation.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Software validation</td><td>Battery managing software</td><td>Test del software di power conditioning e dell’hardware dell’EPS per assicurarsi che non ci siano sbalzi nel power delivery</td><td>EPS_D.4</td><td>EPS_D.4 The EPS shall provide stable buses to all components in order to ensure a correct power distribution.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Safe mode activation</td><td>Battery</td><td>Test dell’attivazione della safe mode</td><td>EPS O.2</td><td>EPS_O.2 The EPS shall be provided with a Safe Mode in case of loss of solar panels&#x27; pointing capability, until a maneuver is done to reestablish the correct pointing.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Dielectric Breakdown</td><td>Solar Panels</td><td>Controlla che non ci siano dispersioni di corrente verso la struttura metallica del pannello.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Mechanical</td><td>Solar panels deplyment mechanism</td><td>Solar Panels</td><td>Verifica il corretto funzionamento del meccanismo di deployment. <br>In particolare:<br>- Margine di coppia/forza: misura l&#x27;energia dei motori o delle molle per garantire che superi di molto gli attriti interni.<br>- Ripetibilità dell&#x27;allineamento: accerta che il pannello, una volta aperto, si blocchi esattamente nella posizione geometrica prevista per puntare il Sole.<br>- Shock di fine corsa: misura l&#x27;impatto meccanico sulla struttura nel momento esatto in cui il pannello completa l&#x27;apertura e si blocca.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Vibrational</td><td>Battery vibrational test</td><td>Battery</td><td>Componente critico di cui è consigliabile verificare la resistenza alla vibrazioni precedentemente al test vibrazionale di sistema integrato.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Thermo-vacuum</td><td>Battery TVAC</td><td>Battery</td><td>Verifica batteria in carica/scarica ai limiti termici operativi, soprattutto in eclissi, low-power mode, safe mode. Verifica sopravvivenza nei range non operativi.</td><td>EPS_D.3<br>TSS_D.3.8<br>TSS_D.3.9<br>TSS_D.3.10</td><td>EPS_D.3 The EPS shall manage shadow phases with the implementation of an energy storage system.<br>TSS_D.3.10: The design shall provide survival heating capability for critical units [TBD], with autonomous activation architecture [TBD].<br>TSS_D.3.8: The TCS shall maintain EPS temperature within [0°C - 50°C].<br>TSS_D.3.9: The spacecraft survival thermal interval in space shall be [-10°C and +60°C] for survival configuration [TBC] and duration [72 h] [TBC].</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></article>
<article class="datasheet" id="sheet-3"><div class="sheet-head"><h2>TTC</h2><p class="model">10 test entries</p></div><div class="sheet-body"><div class="table-wrap"><table class="styled-table"><thead><tr><th>TYPE OF TEST</th><th>TEST NAME</th><th>ITEM VERIFIED</th><th>DESCRIPTION</th><th>REQ</th><th>EXPECTED RESULTS</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>TTC &amp; GNSS</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Performance</td><td>Safran RAFS Stability Characterization</td><td>Atomic clock</td><td>Continuous monitoring of the Rubidium Frequency Standard&#x27;s atomic clock stability using high-precision phase comparators. The objective is to verify that the clock maintains its nominal short-term stability (&lt; 3e-13 at 100s, &lt; 6e-14 at 1000s) and long-term stability (&lt; 3e-14 at 10,000s) under controlled nominal temperatures.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Weak Signal Earth GNSS Acquisition Sensitivity</td><td>NaviMoon Receiver</td><td>Utilizing an RF signal simulator to reproduce the highly attenuated Earth GNSS sidelobe signals received at lunar distances (-180 dBW to -185 dBW). The test will demonstrate that the NaviMoon receiver&#x27;s long coherent integration algorithms can successfully lock and track down to a threshold of 18 dB-Hz.</td><td>F.5<br>P.5<br>TTC_D.1</td><td>F.5 The GNSS payload shall ALSO acquire and track Radio Frequency (RF) signals transmitted by Earth GNSS constellations.<br>P.5 The GNSS receiver shall track Earth-GNSS signal with a carrier-to-noise density C/N0 ≤ 18 dB-Hz (TBC).<br>TCC_D.1 The GNSS payload shall have high-gain antennas and high-sensitivity GNSS receivers optimized for weak signals.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>X-band SSPA Power &amp; Thermal Benchmarking</td><td>X-band SSPA Amplifier</td><td>Characterization of the 30W X-band Solid State Power Amplifier (SSPA) for the TT&amp;C subsystem. Measurements will confirm the nominal Equivalent Isotropically Radiated Power (EIRP) and verify that the power consumption and thermal dissipation remain within the structural budget.</td><td>TTC_D.3</td><td>TTC_D.3 The TTC subsystem shall operate in X-band.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>OBC Functionality Check</td><td>OBC</td><td>Verify OBC capability to receive data from all subsystems and send command to all subsystems, exchange telemetry with Earth GS throught TTC subsystem, control deployment mechanisms and TCS.</td><td>TTC_D.6.1<br>TTC_D.6.2<br>TTC_D.6.3</td><td>TTC_D.6.1 The OBC shall receive data from all subsystems. <br>TTC_D.6.2 The OBC shall be able to send commands to all subsystems. <br>TTC_D.6.3 The OBC shall transmit data to the telecommunication subsystem (TTC).</td></tr><tr><td>Thermo-vacuum</td><td>Clock stability TVAC test</td><td>Atomic clock</td><td>Perform characterization of frequency stability, drift, aging, and phase noise, including performance verification during thermal transients.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Interferance</td><td>Electromagnetic Compatibility &amp; Interference (EMC/EMI)</td><td>Payload antennas: S-band AFS and X-band TT&amp;C</td><td>Rigorous testing to ensure spectral isolation between the payload antennas. The system must verify that simultaneous high-power transmissions from the S-band Augmented Forward Link (AFS) and X-band TT&amp;C do not cause auto-interference, receiver saturation, or harmful intermodulation products in the ultra-sensitive Ka-band ISL terminal.</td><td>TTC_D.1.1</td><td>TCC_D.1.1 The design of the TTC antennas shall ensure EMI/EMC compatibility between GNSS payloads, telecom transmitters and on-board subsystems.</td></tr><tr><td>Performance</td><td>Functionality &amp; Operations validation</td><td>GNSS &amp; TTC Subsystem</td><td>Verify GNSS/TTC Subsystem ability to operate within designed performance, manage temporary link loss (Safe Mode) and GS occultation periods performing autonomous operations and PNT signal generation for at least 96h.</td><td>TTC_O.1<br>TTC_O.2<br>TTC_O.3<br>TTC_O.4</td><td>TTC_O.1 The GNSS/TTC system shall operate continuously with designed performances during the nominal operational phases of the mission.<br>TTC_O.2 The GNSS/TTC system shall operate continuously with degraded performances in the event of temporary link loss (Safe Mode). <br>TTC_O.3 The TTC subsystem shall support autonomous link reconfiguration operations (automatic selection of Earth/ISL relays). <br>TTC_O.4 The payload shall be capable of autonomous operation and PNT signal generation for at least 72 hours [TBC] during GS occultation periods.</td></tr><tr><td>Vibrational</td><td>GNSS &amp; TTC vibrational test</td><td>GNSS &amp; TTC Subsystem</td><td>To stress-test the structural integrity of the systems, certifying survivability against mechanical launch loads.<br>Mechanical shake testing of the integrated satellite structural model, GNSS payload and TTC across launch vehicle profile spectrums. Particular emphasis will be placed on validating that the natural structural frequencies exceed 100 Hz, and that the single-release launch lock mechanism securely immobilizes the S-band antenna and Ka-band APM (Aiming Mechanism) joints under high g-loads.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Thermo-vacuum</td><td>Subsystem Thermal-Vacuum (TVAC) Environmental Cycling</td><td>Qualification Model (QM) / Protoflight Model (PFM)</td><td>To stress-test the structural, thermal, and electrical integrity of the systems, certifying survivability against the harsh cislunar radiation and thermal environment.<br>Subjecting the entire subsystem to extreme thermal cycles inside a vacuum chamber at a pressure of 1e-5 mbar. The operational range will test survival from -15°C up to +20°C to validate the efficiency of the dedicated SSPA heat pipes, ensuring no thermal localized runaway occurs during continuous transmission. <br>Verify signal generation, frequency stability, phase noise, timing, and RF power to ensure they remain within specification at the thermal extremes.</td><td>TTC_O.1</td><td>TTC_O.1 The GNSS/TTC system shall operate continuously with designed performances during the nominal operational phases of the mission</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></article>
<article class="datasheet" id="sheet-4"><div class="sheet-head"><h2>PROP</h2><p class="model">10 test entries</p></div><div class="sheet-body"><div class="table-wrap"><table class="styled-table"><thead><tr><th>TYPE OF TEST</th><th>TEST NAME</th><th>ITEM VERIFIED</th><th>DESCRIPTION</th><th>REQ</th><th>EXPECTED RESULTS</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Performance</td><td>Proof &amp; Leak Test</td><td>Propellant tanks, tubing, fluidic valves</td><td>Perform leak testing on welds and valves to mitigate propellant leakage risks.<br>Expected: <br>- Structural integrity at Proof pressure (1.5 for MEOP)<br>- zero leakage at nominal pressure</td><td>PROP_D.6</td><td>PROP_D.6 The propulsion subsystem shall comply with safety constraints, including a Maximum Expected Operating Pressure (MEOP) of [70] ± 0.3 bar and a Factor of Safety (FOS) on tank Yield stress ≥ [2].​</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Electrical insulation and continuity</td><td>Harness, Electronic Control Unit (ECU), pressure sensors and thermocouples</td><td>Mandatory for critical units: verify harness and electronics to prevent high-voltage breakdown and inrush currents.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Mass evaluation</td><td>PROP Subsystem</td><td>Evaluate mass of PROP Subsystem.</td><td>PROP_D.3</td><td>PROP_D.3 The Propulsion System mass shall not exceed 80 ± 10 kg [TBC].</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Volume evaluation</td><td>PROP Subsystem</td><td>Evaluate volume of PROP Subsystem.</td><td>PROP_D.4</td><td>PROP_D.4 The propulsion System envelope shall fit within a volume of 1X1X0.7 ± 0.15 m³.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Orbital manouvers verification</td><td>PROP Subsystem</td><td>Verify capability to satisfied required change of orbital position.</td><td>PROP_D.1.1<br>PROP_D.1.3<br>PROP_D.1.4<br>PROP_D.7.1<br>PROP_O.1<br>PROP_O.2</td><td>PROP_D.1.1 The Propulsive System shall be able to perform orbital maneuvers. <br>PROP_D.1.3 The Propulsive System should be able to perform the station keeping in case of failure of internal actuators. <br>PROP_D.1.4 The Propulsive System shall be able to perform End of Life (EoL) maneuvers. <br>PROP_D.7.4 The Propulsion System shall be able to provide a DELTA-V of 500 m/s for deorbiting [TBC]. <br>PROP_D.7.1 The Propulsion System shall provide a total DELTA-V of 650 m/s for the entire mission<br>PROP_O.1 The propulsion subsystem shall autonomously perform station keeping outside the transmission and acquisition windows. <br>PROP_O.2 The propulsion subsystem shall be capable of executing autonomous operations (e.g., deorbiting) in the event of prolonged radio signal loss or external failures.</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanical</td><td>Interfaces validation</td><td>Mechanical interface flange and structural anchoring points, data and power connectors, ADCS/OBDH subsystem interfaces</td><td>Verify nominal operation of PCU/on-board electronics electrical interface and ADCS/OBDH interfaces.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Thrust Alignment</td><td>Thruster (B1/B20)</td><td>Perform millimetric thrust vector alignment measurement with respect to the satellite structural interfaces.<br>Expected thrust vector misalignment ≤0.5° with respect to the geometric centre of mass of the interface.</td><td>PROP_D.10</td><td>PROP_D.10 The Propulsion System thrust vector misalignment error shall be ≤ [0.5] ± 0.01 degrees for main thruster and ≤ [2] ± 0.01 degrees for RCS thrusters. ​</td></tr><tr><td>Vibration</td><td>PROP Subsystem vibration Test</td><td>Module structural support framework, loaded propellant tank structural mounts, fluidic line joints/interconnections</td><td>Perform launch vehicle mechanical load simulation (sine and random vibration testing) focusing on loaded tank interfaces.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Thermo-vacuum</td><td>Propulsion TVAC (subsystem level)</td><td>Propellant fluidic line heaters, in-line pressure and temperature sensors</td><td>Critical Subsystem: pre-integration subsystem test (highest severity and cycle count).<br>Monitor:<br>- Heater duty cycles: verify prevention of line freezing.<br>- N2O thermal decomposition: verify line temperatures do not trigger premature N2O decomposition.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Plume Characterization</td><td>Thruster (B1/B20) (impact on spacecraft structure, appendices)</td><td>Perform plume expansion angle measurement for solar array and antenna plume impingement model validation (propulsion component recommended).</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></article>
<article class="datasheet" id="sheet-5"><div class="sheet-head"><h2>THERMO-STRUCTURE</h2><p class="model">1 test entries</p></div><div class="sheet-body"><div class="table-wrap"><table class="styled-table"><thead><tr><th>TYPE OF TEST</th><th>TEST NAME</th><th>ITEM VERIFIED</th><th>DESCRIPTION</th><th>REQ</th><th>EXPECTED RESULTS</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Performance</td><td>Heater control test</td><td>Active thermal control components (heaters)</td><td>Perform thermal control system verification, including thermostats, heater lines, duty cycles, flight software logic, redundancy management, and hot/cold temperature thresholds.</td><td>TSS_D.3.3<br>TSS_D.3.4<br>TSS_D.3.10<br>TSS_O.1.2</td><td>TSS_D.3.3: Temperature measurement shall be performed at a minimal frequency of 1 Hz.<br>TSS_D.3.4: The TCS shall actively control spacecraft thermal state by commanding heaters.<br>TSS_D.3.10: The design shall provide survival heating capability for critical units [TBD], with autonomous activation architecture [TBD].<br>TSS_O.1.2 Heaters shall be commandable from GS with set-point/enable limits to prevent unsafe commanding.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></article></main><footer>SPACE SYSTEM LABORATORY – Preliminary Design Review · May 2026</footer></div></body></html>
